1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,590
It's two in the morning.

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:04,290
Something creaks near your front door.

3
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:06,141
Your eyes open.

4
00:00:06,141 --> 00:00:08,560
You lie still and listen.

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,972
There it is again.

6
00:00:10,972 --> 00:00:13,950
A small, dry sound from the hallway.

7
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:18,520
You get out of bed, cross the room and check
the lock.

8
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:20,250
It's secure.

9
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:26,130
You stand there another moment, looking into
the dark, until the sound comes again.

10
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:28,630
It's the floor.

11
00:00:28,630 --> 00:00:30,790
You were the one making it.

12
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:36,672
You go back to bed, slightly annoyed with
yourself, and pull the covers up.

13
00:00:36,672 --> 00:00:38,880
The door is locked.

14
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,000
The walls are solid.

15
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,500
Eventually, you close your eyes.

16
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,310
Now take away the lock.

17
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:49,124
Take away the door.

18
00:00:49,124 --> 00:00:54,950
Take away the quiet room, the mattress, the
blanket, and the light switch beside your
bed.

19
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:56,940
How would you sleep?

20
00:00:56,940 --> 00:01:02,320
For much of human history, darkness arrived
without a key to turn.

21
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:07,473
But that didn't mean people simply collapsed
wherever they stood and hoped for the best.

22
00:01:07,473 --> 00:01:11,755
To understand the night, first look at the
world around them.

23
00:01:11,755 --> 00:01:19,830
Two hundred thousand years ago, in what is
now southern Africa, daylight moves across a
rocky slope.

24
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:22,340
Grasses bend in the breeze.

25
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:28,433
Beyond them are patches of bush and open
woodland, with trees scattered across the
landscape.

26
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:32,815
As the sun drops, the rocks hold a little
warmth.

27
00:01:32,815 --> 00:01:34,780
The shadows stretch.

28
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:38,360
The paths through the vegetation become
harder to see.

29
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:42,630
Near a broad shelter in the cliff, a
community gathers.

30
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:45,310
Adults return from different tasks.

31
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:47,190
Children move between them.

32
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:51,910
Grass is carried into the shelter; hands
clear a space on the ground.

33
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:56,170
At the edge of the gathering, people settle
near the warmth of a fire.

34
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:58,540
There is movement everywhere.

35
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:00,910
Somebody shifts closer to the light.

36
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:02,600
Someone else makes room.

37
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:06,920
A child is reluctant to settle; an adult
draws them close.

38
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:11,230
The day hasn't ended all at once just
because the sun has gone down.

39
00:02:11,230 --> 00:02:16,860
The precise size of any particular ancient
group is difficult to know.

40
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:25,037
But the archaeological traces at one South
African site give us a remarkable glimpse of
a place people returned to: Border Cave,

41
00:02:25,037 --> 00:02:27,090
in the Lebombo Mountains.

42
00:02:27,090 --> 00:02:33,500
And at the back of that shelter,
archaeologists found something unexpectedly
familiar.

43
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,110
A bed.

44
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:38,720
Or, more precisely, traces of many layers of
bedding.

45
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:45,910
The remains are subtle now: ancient
fragments of broad-leaved grass, identified
by careful analysis.

46
00:02:45,910 --> 00:02:51,300
Some bedding at Border Cave dates to around
two hundred thousand years ago.

47
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,300
Imagine the preparation before nightfall.

48
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:59,845
Grass gathered in bundles, carried back to
the shelter and spread where people could
rest.

49
00:02:59,845 --> 00:03:03,160
A softer surface between a body and the
ground.

50
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:05,640
Not a mattress with a warranty.

51
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:10,384
But certainly better than finding a sharp
stone with your back at three in the
morning.

52
00:03:10,384 --> 00:03:12,890
The grass wasn't the only thing underfoot.

53
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:16,890
Researchers also found layers of ash
associated with the bedding.

54
00:03:16,890 --> 00:03:20,817
Sometimes that ash appears to have come from
older bedding that had burned.

55
00:03:20,817 --> 00:03:23,680
Why place fresh grass over ash?

56
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:28,020
One possibility is that it helped create a
cleaner, insulated base.

57
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:33,140
The researchers also suggested the fine ash
might have discouraged crawling pests.

58
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:38,149
That part is an interpretation, not a label
left behind by the people who made it.

59
00:03:38,149 --> 00:03:41,300
But it points to a problem that is easy to
overlook.

60
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:45,533
The danger of the night wasn't only
something large moving beyond the fire.

61
00:03:45,533 --> 00:03:49,420
It could be something small enough to
disappear into the bedding.

62
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,210
Tiny stone fragments were found among the
grass as well.

63
00:03:53,210 --> 00:03:58,630
The same surface that offered somewhere to
rest may also have been a place to sit and
work.

64
00:03:58,630 --> 00:04:00,555
A sleeping place.

65
00:04:00,555 --> 00:04:01,850
A working place.

66
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:06,726
A piece of the landscape brought indoors, if
a shelter can be called indoors.

67
00:04:06,726 --> 00:04:10,850
But a bed still leaves one problem unsolved.

68
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:14,010
What happens beyond the edge of the light?

69
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:19,334
At Border Cave, evidence of fires appears
through a long sequence of occupations.

70
00:04:19,334 --> 00:04:24,310
For the people using such a place, a fire
could offer warmth and light.

71
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:28,144
In some circumstances it might also help
discourage animals from approaching.

72
00:04:28,144 --> 00:04:31,380
But it could not turn night into day.

73
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:34,630
And it was never a guarantee that nothing
would come near.

74
00:04:34,630 --> 00:04:42,000
Picture the firelight reaching the people
nearest it: faces, hands, the pale edges of
grass.

75
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,360
Farther back, rock disappears into shadow.

76
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:49,932
Beyond the shelter mouth, the light weakens
until the bushes are only shapes.

77
00:04:49,932 --> 00:04:54,000
The surrounding world doesn't stop when
people settle down.

78
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,520
Wind passes through dry stems.

79
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,490
Insects continue their work.

80
00:04:58,490 --> 00:05:02,250
Somewhere beyond the slope, an animal moves
through vegetation.

81
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:05,680
A sound can travel a long way in the dark.

82
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:07,570
Its owner might be harmless.

83
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:09,120
Or not.

84
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:12,470
From inside the shelter, the difference
isn't always obvious.

85
00:05:12,470 --> 00:05:20,830
People could choose where to rest, use the
shelter and tend a fire, but even a
carefully prepared place had limits.

86
00:05:20,830 --> 00:05:22,600
Smoke could irritate.

87
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:24,950
A change in weather could bring cold or
rain.

88
00:05:24,950 --> 00:05:29,400
A sleeping person still had to let go of
attention for hours.

89
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:35,483
And that's the strange bargain at the heart
of every night: to recover from the day,

90
00:05:35,483 --> 00:05:38,830
they had to become less aware of the world
around them.

91
00:05:38,830 --> 00:05:42,780
So what if a sound came from the grass
outside?

92
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:45,900
Imagine a scrape beyond the shelter.

93
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:47,700
Then silence.

94
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:49,830
Then the sound again.

95
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:54,540
Inside, not everyone responds in the same
way.

96
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:56,300
Some remain asleep.

97
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,170
Others lift their heads.

98
00:05:58,170 --> 00:06:02,230
Eyes turn toward the entrance, where the
firelight ends.

99
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:07,200
In daylight, a moving branch is just a
moving branch.

100
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:12,730
At night, with the source hidden, it can
hold a whole collection of possibilities.

101
00:06:12,730 --> 00:06:15,130
A gust of wind.

102
00:06:15,130 --> 00:06:17,120
A small animal.

103
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:18,320
Something larger.

104
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:19,930
The scrape stops.

105
00:06:19,930 --> 00:06:21,720
The night continues.

106
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:26,170
People settle again, but perhaps not all at
the same moment.

107
00:06:26,170 --> 00:06:31,163
It's easy to picture one assigned guard
standing at the entrance until sunrise.

108
00:06:31,163 --> 00:06:35,490
And it's possible that groups used watches
in some situations.

109
00:06:35,490 --> 00:06:40,567
But an ancient layer of grass cannot tell us
that a guard stood there every night.

110
00:06:40,567 --> 00:06:42,660
There is another possibility.

111
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:49,530
A group does not need to move through the
night like a single creature, sleeping and
waking at exactly the same time.

112
00:06:49,530 --> 00:06:54,423
Researchers have found a striking example of
that among people living today.

113
00:06:54,423 --> 00:06:57,088
In a study in Tanzania,

114
00:06:57,088 --> 00:07:02,187
researchers used wrist-worn activity
monitors to estimate the sleep of
thirty-three Hadza

115
00:07:02,187 --> 00:07:03,683
adults over twenty nights.

116
00:07:03,683 --> 00:07:09,720
The Hadza are a living community with their
own history; the study tells us about its
participants,

117
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,961
not a direct record of ancient sleep.

118
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:18,985
The researchers found that the monitored
adults were all estimated to be asleep at
the same time during only

119
00:07:18,985 --> 00:07:21,250
eighteen one-minute intervals across the
study.

120
00:07:21,250 --> 00:07:25,830
Eighteen minutes across twenty nights, not
eighteen minutes every night.

121
00:07:25,830 --> 00:07:29,600
Their sleep overlapped, but it didn't line
up perfectly.

122
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:31,460
Some people fell asleep later.

123
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:32,840
Some woke earlier.

124
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:35,820
Some were active during the night for
reasons of their own.

125
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:42,560
Researchers have proposed that differences
in sleeping times could sometimes give a
group more opportunities to notice a threat.

126
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:45,530
This idea is known as the sentinel
hypothesis.

127
00:07:45,530 --> 00:07:50,020
Notice the difference between the
observation and the explanation.

128
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:57,680
The monitors estimated whether people were
asleep or active; they did not prove that
every awake person was keeping watch.

129
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:01,840
And the study cannot establish exactly how
people slept at Border Cave.

130
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:05,940
Still, it opens up a different way to think
about the night.

131
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:11,462
A sleeping community isn't necessarily a
room where every pair of eyes closes at
once.

132
00:08:11,462 --> 00:08:15,040
Back at the shelter, the fire has burned
lower.

133
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:17,820
The space nearest the entrance is darker
now.

134
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:20,860
Beyond it, the landscape is still moving.

135
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:24,180
And the next challenge might be closer than
the bushes.

136
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:28,920
Even without a threat outside, a night on
the ground takes preparation.

137
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,290
The surface beneath the body matters.

138
00:08:31,290 --> 00:08:36,312
So do the air, the wind, the heat of the
fire, and the space around other sleepers.

139
00:08:36,312 --> 00:08:39,000
Some may want to be near the warmth.

140
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,860
Others may shift away when the smoke changes
direction.

141
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:45,370
People make room, adjust their positions and
settle again.

142
00:08:45,370 --> 00:08:50,070
A crowded place can be reassuring; it can
also be full of elbows.

143
00:08:50,070 --> 00:08:53,030
And what did they wear against the night
air?

144
00:08:53,030 --> 00:08:59,660
Clothing is difficult to trace this far back
because materials such as skin and plant
fibre usually decay.

145
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:03,080
The exact garments worn at Border Cave are
not known.

146
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:05,420
There are later clues elsewhere.

147
00:09:05,420 --> 00:09:07,267
At a cave in Morocco,

148
00:09:07,267 --> 00:09:13,921
researchers found bone tools and marks on
animal bones dating to roughly ninety to one
hundred and twenty thousand years ago.

149
00:09:13,921 --> 00:09:20,930
Together, they suggest people processed
hides and fur; clothing is one possible use,
though not the only one.

150
00:09:20,930 --> 00:09:24,640
That discovery is from a different place and
a later time.

151
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:28,639
It doesn't tell us the cut or appearance of
anyone's clothing at Border Cave.

152
00:09:28,639 --> 00:09:34,380
What it does show is how much thought can be
hidden inside an ordinary material.

153
00:09:34,380 --> 00:09:40,395
A skin, a length of plant fibre, a layer of
grass: each could have many possible uses.

154
00:09:40,395 --> 00:09:44,601
Which of them were used on one particular
night is a harder question.

155
00:09:44,601 --> 00:09:48,776
The people at the shelter have another few
hours before dawn.

156
00:09:48,776 --> 00:09:50,240
The fire settles.

157
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:51,815
The ground cools.

158
00:09:51,815 --> 00:09:57,110
Around them, some sleep deeply; others may
wake, shift and lie down again.

159
00:09:57,110 --> 00:10:00,300
Then the darkness begins to change.

160
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:04,610
At first, the shelter mouth is only a paler
shape against the rock.

161
00:10:04,610 --> 00:10:08,210
Then the surrounding grasses begin to
separate from the shadows.

162
00:10:08,210 --> 00:10:10,410
The outlines of trees return.

163
00:10:10,410 --> 00:10:17,117
The land that seemed so uncertain in the
dark becomes visible again, one detail at a
time.

164
00:10:17,117 --> 00:10:18,570
The community stirs.

165
00:10:18,570 --> 00:10:22,935
People rise from the grass, step around one
another and move toward the day.

166
00:10:22,935 --> 00:10:28,140
The place that held sleepers through the
night becomes a place for work and movement
again.

167
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:37,560
Long after those people were gone, traces
remained: grass and ash, signs of fire, and
small fragments of worked stone.

168
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:43,620
They cannot tell us every sound of the night
or the exact way the community responded to
it.

169
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:50,460
But they offer a different picture from the
idea that ancient people simply lay down in
the dark and took their chances.

170
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:52,560
They could prepare a place.

171
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:54,720
They could use the landscape.

172
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:56,640
They could be near other people.

173
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:59,800
And, even then, the night could surprise
them.

174
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:04,260
Back in your room, the floor creaks again.

175
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:07,210
This time, you know what made it.

176
00:11:07,210 --> 00:11:12,900
You check the door once more anyway, return
to bed, and let the quiet settle.

177
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:15,860
The surroundings have changed.

178
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:17,620
The question hasn't.

179
00:11:17,620 --> 00:11:22,200
Can you go back to sleep?
